To install critic2, please follow the instructions in:

https://aoterodelaroza.github.io/critic2/installation/

Windows builds
--------------

The Windows package is produced by cross-compiling from Linux with MinGW-w64
and packaging with CPack. The result is a self-contained tree -- shipped both as
a portable .zip and as an NSIS installer .exe -- that bundles every required DLL,
so it runs on a stock Windows machine with no extra dependencies.

Almost all of this is automated. If you only want to build the distribution,
skip to section 3; sections 1 and 2 explain what goes into it and why.


1. Which libraries end up in the package

   critic2 can use the libraries below. None of them are required: every one is
   detected independently and silently left out if absent, so a package can be
   built with any subset. The "how" column is what the cross build needs, since
   only two of them publish binaries that a MinGW cross build can consume.

   IN THE PACKAGE, from prebuilt binaries:

   * GLFW 3 -- windowing and OpenGL context; required for the GUI, not used by
     the command line. The glfw.org "Windows pre-compiled binaries (64-bit)"
     ship a MinGW import library in lib-mingw-w64/.
     https://www.glfw.org/download

   * OpenBLAS -- fast external LAPACK/BLAS. The project publishes a MinGW build
     as the "OpenBLAS-<version>-x64.zip" asset. Without it critic2 falls back to
     its slower bundled reference LAPACK, and xtb (below) cannot be built at all.
     https://github.com/OpenMathLib/OpenBLAS

   * Mesa -- software OpenGL, used as a fallback on machines with no GPU or
     OpenGL < 3.3 (old hardware, Remote Desktop, GPU-less VMs). Take the MinGW
     "release" package and use the directory holding opengl32.dll,
     libgallium_wgl.dll, libglapi.dll and dxil.dll. Use a 24.x release if you
     care about Windows 7: newer Mesa imports Win8+ symbols and will not load.
     https://github.com/pal1000/mesa-dist-win

   IN THE PACKAGE, cross-built from source (no MinGW binaries exist):

   * FreeType -- higher-quality GUI font rendering. imgui needs only its core
     API, so it is built as a minimal DLL with zlib/bzip2/png/harfbuzz/brotli
     disabled and therefore no dependencies of its own. Without it the GUI uses
     imgui's lower-quality built-in rasterizer.

   * readline -- interactive command-line editing and history. GNU readline
     needs termcap/termios and does not build for Windows; the substitute is
     wineditline, a BSD-licensed, readline-API-compatible library whose edit.dll
     exports exactly the readline/add_history/read_history/write_history
     functions critic2 uses. It is installed under a readline-shaped prefix
     (lib/libreadline.dll.a, include/readline/) so the stock FindREADLINE finds
     it. Without it the CLI falls back to the plain Windows console editor.
     https://sourceforge.net/projects/mingweditline/

   * libxc -- exchange-correlation energies and potentials (the LIBXC keyword).
     Must be built with ENABLE_FORTRAN=ON, and with the same gfortran that
     compiles critic2: critic2 uses the Fortran 2003 interface, and .mod files
     are not portable between compilers or major versions. That is also why a
     prebuilt binary could never have served here.
     https://gitlab.com/libxc/libxc

   * nlopt -- global optimization, used by COMPAREVC and by the XRPD profile
     fits. Must be built with NLOPT_FORTRAN=ON: critic2 uses the F77 interface,
     and nlopt.f is only generated when that is on. The C++ half is left out --
     the algorithms critic2 asks for (SLSQP, MLSL_LDS, PRAXIS, CCSAQ, MMA) are
     all in the C core, and omitting it keeps libstdc++ off the CLI's DLL list.
     https://github.com/stevengj/nlopt

   * libcint -- molecular integrals over Gaussian functions. Needs
     -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=-std=gnu17: it declares old-style function pointers
     (int (*f)()), which gcc >= 15 rejects under its default -std=gnu23.
     https://github.com/sunqm/libcint

   * xtb -- GFN-FF energies and forces for the interactive dynamics. critic2
     drives it through its C API only, so no Fortran module files have to match.
     Needs the same -std=gnu17 as libcint (symmetry_i.c defines its own
     true/false), LAPACK, and two build-time-only libraries of its own,
     mctc-lib and test-drive. Those must be supplied rather than left to xtb's
     FetchContent fallback, which clones mctc-lib's HEAD and then collides with
     the alias target mctc-lib now defines itself; they are built static and
     absorbed into libxtb.dll, so nothing extra is shipped.
     https://github.com/grimme-lab/xtb

   NOT IN THE PACKAGE:

   * HDF5 (reading phonopy HDF5 vibration data in the GUI) -- HDF5 decides its
     type conversions by running probe programs on the target, which a cross
     build cannot do. A native MSYS2 build (bottom of this file) can use the
     mingw-w64-x86_64-hdf5 package instead.

   * tblite (GFN1/GFN2-xTB energies and forces) -- this one does cross-build,
     but it needs a five-library chain first (mctc-lib, toml-f, multicharge,
     s-dftd3, dftd4) and two upstream workarounds: its driver executable will
     not link against a DLL that re-exports its own static dependencies, and its
     install rules have no RUNTIME DESTINATION, so libtblite.dll never leaves
     the build tree and has to be copied into the prefix by hand. Enable it as
     described in section 4 if you are willing to do that.

   * OpenMP needs no download at all -- libgomp ships with the toolchain -- and
     is enabled by default.


2. Host prerequisites (Debian/Ubuntu package names)

   * MinGW-w64 toolchain, POSIX thread model. The win32 thread model corrupts
     memory when the GUI spawns threads, so the posix compilers are required:

       apt install gfortran-mingw-w64-x86-64 g++-mingw-w64-x86-64 gcc-mingw-w64-x86-64

     Note gfortran is a separate package from gcc, and libxc, nlopt and xtb all
     need it. Keep the mingw-w64 runtime in sync with the compiler by using
     "apt full-upgrade", or upgrade the mingw-w64* packages explicitly.

   * cmake, curl, unzip, p7zip-full (7z, for the Mesa archive):

       apt install cmake curl unzip p7zip-full

   * NSIS, only if you want the installer (provides makensis):

       apt install nsis


3. Fetch the dependencies

   One command downloads the prebuilt libraries, cross-builds the rest, and
   prints a ready-to-paste recipe -- the configure line with every path and
   -DUSE_* flag already filled in, plus the build and package commands:

       tools/fetch-windows-deps.sh [DEST]

   DEST defaults to ./windows-deps. Everything is cached: a second run rebuilds
   nothing, and an interrupted build resumes rather than starting over. Anything
   that cannot be built is reported and simply turned OFF in the printed recipe,
   so the script always produces a working recipe for the libraries you actually
   have. Delete a source directory under DEST to force a clean rebuild of it.

   Set FETCH_MESA / FETCH_LAPACK / FETCH_FREETYPE / FETCH_READLINE /
   FETCH_LIBXC / FETCH_NLOPT / FETCH_LIBCINT / FETCH_XTB to 0 to skip a
   dependency, JOBS to cap the compile parallelism, and GLFW_VERSION /
   MESA_VERSION / OPENBLAS_VERSION / FREETYPE_VERSION / WINEDITLINE_VERSION /
   LIBXC_VERSION / NLOPT_VERSION / LIBCINT_VERSION / XTB_VERSION /
   MCTCLIB_VERSION / TESTDRIVE_VERSION to pick versions.

   Then run the three commands it prints and you are done. The rest of this
   section describes those commands, in case you want to assemble them by hand.


4. Configure

   If you did not use the script, set the paths yourself. Every prefix below is
   optional except GLFW (and only for a GUI build); each contains bin/, lib/ and
   include/ unless noted:

     GLFW=/path/to/glfw-3.4.bin.WIN64         # GUI only
     MESA=/path/to/mesa/x64                   # software-OpenGL fallback
     OPENBLAS=/path/to/openblas-x64
     FREETYPE=/path/to/freetype-mingw
     READLINE=/path/to/wineditline-mingw
     LIBXC=/path/to/libxc-mingw
     NLOPT=/path/to/nlopt-mingw
     LIBCINT=/path/to/libcint-mingw           # DLL is in lib/, not bin/
     XTB=/path/to/xtb-mingw

     cmake -S . -B build-win \
       -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/toolchains/x86_64-w64-mingw32.cmake \
       -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
       -DENABLE_GUI=ON -DENABLE_OPENMP=ON \
       -DGLFW3_INCLUDE_DIR=$GLFW/include \
       -DGLFW3_LIBRARY=$GLFW/lib-mingw-w64/libglfw3dll.a \
       -DMESA_DIR=$MESA \
       -DUSE_EXTERNAL_LAPACK=ON -DBLA_VENDOR=OpenBLAS \
       -DUSE_FREETYPE=ON -DUSE_READLINE=ON \
       -DUSE_LIBXC=ON -DUSE_NLOPT=ON -DUSE_LIBCINT=ON -DUSE_XTB=ON \
       -DCRITIC2_FIND_ROOT="$OPENBLAS;$FREETYPE;$READLINE;$LIBXC;$NLOPT;$LIBCINT;$XTB" \
       -DUSE_HDF5=OFF -DUSE_TBLITE=OFF

   Set the -DUSE_* flag to OFF for every prefix you did not build, and drop it
   from CRITIC2_FIND_ROOT. Leaving a shell variable unset instead makes its
   find-root entry empty; the build then quietly drops that library rather than
   failing, and you ship a package missing a feature you thought you enabled.
   (The recipe the script prints already gets this right.)

   Verify what you got before packaging -- the configure summary lists every
   library, and the built binary reports the same set on startup:

     features: LAPACK(external) LibXC-7.0.0 CINT Readline NLopt xtb GUI(freetype)

   Notes:
   * Drop -DMESA_DIR to omit the software fallback (the GUI then needs the target
     machine to provide OpenGL 3.3 itself).
   * For a command-line-only package use -DENABLE_GUI=OFF and omit the GLFW,
     MESA and FREETYPE options.
   * -DGLFW3_ROOT=$GLFW also works when the layout is standard; the explicit
     INCLUDE_DIR/LIBRARY above is reliable for the glfw.org WIN64 binary dist,
     whose import library lives in lib-mingw-w64/. Use libglfw3dll.a (not
     libglfw3.a) so glfw3.dll is bundled.
   * -DCRITIC2_FIND_ROOT is a ';'-separated list of extra prefixes added to the
     cross find-root; each is searched for import libraries (<prefix>/lib) and
     for DLLs to bundle (<prefix>/bin and <prefix>/lib).
   * -DBLA_VENDOR=OpenBLAS tells CMake's FindLAPACK to use the OpenBLAS in
     CRITIC2_FIND_ROOT. To use critic2's bundled reference LAPACK instead, drop
     it and set -DUSE_EXTERNAL_LAPACK=OFF -- but note xtb then cannot be built.
   * See the header of cmake/toolchains/x86_64-w64-mingw32.cmake for more.


5. Build

     cmake --build build-win -j

   This produces build-win/src/critic2.exe (console CLI) and, for a GUI build,
   build-win/src/critic2-gui.exe (windowed GUI).


6. Package. Run CPack from the build directory. All required runtime DLLs (the
   compiler runtime, GLFW, and every optional library used) are detected and
   bundled automatically.

     cd build-win
     cpack -G ZIP     # portable  -> critic2-<version>-win64.zip
     cpack -G NSIS    # installer -> critic2-<version>-win64.exe

   or produce both at once (also what a bare "cpack" does here when makensis is
   present):

     cpack -G "ZIP;NSIS"

   Package layout: bin/ holds the executables (native OpenGL) with their runtime
   DLLs; if MESA_DIR was set, a sibling bin-mesa/ holds a second GUI executable
   next to Mesa's software OpenGL (it shares bin/'s runtime DLLs, so they are not
   duplicated). The root has small launcher executables -- double-clickable, with
   the critic2 icon and no console flash -- that run the right binary relative to
   their own location (so they work wherever the package is unpacked):

     critic2.exe                        the GUI (native OpenGL)
     critic2 (console).exe              the command line
     critic2 (software rendering).exe   the GUI forced onto Mesa/llvmpipe, for
                                        machines with no GPU or OpenGL < 3.3

   The .zip is portable: unzip it anywhere and double-click a launcher; critic2
   finds its data next to the executables, with no environment variables. The
   .exe installer puts critic2 under Program Files, adds bin to PATH, and creates
   Start-Menu and desktop shortcuts.

Native build in an MSYS2 shell (no toolchain file needed):

    cmake -B build -G "MSYS Makefiles" [-DENABLE_GUI=ON ...]
    cmake --build build -j

The regression tests are POSIX-only and are skipped on Windows.
